  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Osugacestat may interact with VMRC-MELG through multiple pathways, all involving the T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3. This interaction is supported by the presence of multiple targets, including CD3, which is a known target of Osugacestat. The compound 2-(1a,4-dimethyl-3,3a,4,5,6,7,7a,7b-octahydro-2H-naphtho[1,2-b]oxiren-7-yl)prop-2-enoic acid, which is also a target of Osugacestat, directly links to VMRC-MELG. Similarly, another compound, Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, (3beta)-, which is also a target of Osugacestat, has a direct interaction with VMRC-MELG. Additionally, the compound (2R,3R,4S,5R)-2-[[(2S)-7-hydroxy-1-(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)-3-(hydroxymethyl)-6-methoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalen-2-yl]methoxy]oxane-3,4,5-triol, which is a target of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2A1, also interacts with VMRC-MELG. These findings collectively suggest that Osugacestat may act as a modulator of VMRC-MELG through various molecular pathways, potentially influencing cellular processes involving T-cell activation and signaling. The evidence is consistent across different targets, indicating a possible broad mechanism of action, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated."
